From 1c7f1091b3520de292e81373f5fd14f31bff383b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilko Iliev <iliev@ronetix.at>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:17:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sama5d3xmb_gmac.dtsi: use rgmii-id phy-mode for the KSZ9131
 GMAC PHY

The stock Atmel reference motherboard dtsi sets phy-mode = "rgmii" on
macb0 (GMAC) and relies on board-level RGMII clock-delay compensation
tuned for the original Micrel KSZ9021 PHY, expressed as
*-skew-ps device-tree properties on the ethernet-phy child nodes (those
properties are specific to the KSZ9021 driver's own DT binding and are
not read by the KSZ9131 driver at all -- confirmed by dmesg on real
hardware: the KSZ9131 driver binds correctly via MDIO ID auto-detect,
'PHY ... driver [Microchip KSZ9131 Gigabit PHY]', so the skew-ps
properties are silently unused).

On SAMA5D3X-CM, the Microchip KSZ9131 GMAC PHY (MDIO address 1, RC
reset only, no dedicated reset GPIO) trains link fine as-is (dmesg:
'Link is Up - 1Gbps/Full') but passes zero traffic in either direction
(a DHCP client sends discovers, gets 0 replies, RX packets stays 0) --
the classic symptom of a missing RGMII clock/data delay: autonegotiation
survives a misaligned RGMII data path (it doesn't depend on it), but
actual frame data does not.

'rgmii-id' tells the MAC-side RGMII delay to be provided entirely by
the PHY's own internal delay lines (both RX and TX), which is what the
KSZ9131 driver's ksz9131_config_init() enables for this phy-mode value.
This matches a board with no external PCB-level clock-delay
compensation, which is the case here (RC-only reset, no other special
Ethernet provisioning mentioned).

Signed-off-by: Ilko Iliev <iliev@ronetix.at>
---
 arch/arm/boot/dts/sama5d3xmb_gmac.dtsi | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/sama5d3xmb_gmac.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/sama5d3xmb_gmac.dtsi
index d750da3..36d2845 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/sama5d3xmb_gmac.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/sama5d3xmb_gmac.dtsi
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@ / {
 	ahb {
 		apb {
 			macb0: ethernet@f0028000 {
-				phy-mode = "rgmii";
+				phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
 				#address-cells = <1>;
 				#size-cells = <0>;
 
